Reference app
Study the reference app: the retrieval pipeline with a min_score floor and citations — read app/rag.py and app/store.py at tag l06-rag. To see exactly what RAG adds on top of a plain index, run git diff l05-index l06-rag. Try it live: <https://rag-demo.nat-d.uk>. It's a reference to learn from, not a homework answer.

Full-stack version — see every piece wired together
The reference app above is deliberately compact (a few files). For the version we walk through component by component — a real vector database, a layered backend, and a UI that shows each step — study the full-stack RAG demo. It's the same ideas as this lecture, assembled into a runnable app:

Postgres + pgvector as the vector store (its own Docker image), with an Alembic migration that creates the vector(1024) column and an HNSW cosine index.
A FastAPI backend in the course's router → service → repository layering, where SQLAlchemy runs the cosine-similarity search (ORDER BY embedding <=> query).
A Svelte frontend with two pages: Ingest — upload a file and watch how it's chunked (with the overlap highlighted) before it's embedded and stored — and Ask — see a question get embedded, retrieved (ranked chunks + scores), augmented into the system prompt, and answered.
git clone it, add your personal key, and docker compose up. The README has a step-by-step setup guide and a guided reading order for the files — start there, then read the code in the order it suggests to trace one document from upload to grounded answer.